Difference Between Synchronous and Asynchronous File Operations

File operations are used to read from or write data to files stored on secondary memory. Based on the execution behavior, file operations are classified as synchronous and asynchronous.

Synchronous File Operations

Synchronous file operations execute in a blocking manner. 
In this approach, the program waits until the file operation is completed before moving on to the next instruction.
During this time, the execution of the program is halted. 
This method is simple to implement but may reduce performance, especially when dealing with large files or slow I/O operations.

Asynchronous File Operations

Asynchronous file operations execute in a non-blocking manner. 
The program does not wait for the file operation to complete and continues executing the remaining instructions.
Once the file operation is finished, a callback function, promise, or event handler is used to process the result.
This approach improves performance and is widely used in modern applications
